Makes sense to me.  Thanks @JonathanBrouwer for pushing this forward.  I propose we do it:

@rfcbot fcp merge lang

One thing I'll mention is that we do use `unsafe` both to create and to discharge safety obligations.  So far, all the `unsafe` attributes discharge obligations.  If we both 1) ever wanted to create a safety obligation with an `unsafe` attribute and 2) rethink whether `unsafe_code` should apply to creating safety obligations (e.g., https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/148651, which we discussed without resolution in our meeting on 2026-01-14), then maybe this would need to get a bit more complicated.  Not a problem today.

Here's a benchmark illustrating the second performance reason (byte-buffered PRNGs):

```rust
#!/usr/bin/env -S cargo +nightly -Zscript bench --manifest-path
---cargo
[dependencies]
chacha8rand = { version = "=0.1.2", features = ["std"] }
---
#![feature(test, random)]
extern crate test;

use std::random::{RandomSource, DefaultRandomSource};
use test::Bencher;
use chacha8rand::ChaCha8Rand;

fn rng() -> ChaCha8Rand {
    let mut seed = [0; 32];
    DefaultRandomSource.fill_bytes(&mut seed);
    ChaCha8Rand::new(&seed)
}

#[bench]
fn read_u32_direct(b: &mut Bencher) {
    let mut r = rng();
    b.iter(|| r.read_u32());
}

#[bench]
fn read_u32_as_bytes(b: &mut Bencher) {
    let mut r = rng();
    b.iter(|| {
        let mut buf = [0; 4];
        r.read_bytes(&mut buf);
        u32::from_le_bytes(buf)
    });
}

#[bench]
fn read_u64_direct(b: &mut Bencher) {
    let mut r = rng();
    b.iter(|| r.read_u64());
}

#[bench]
fn read_u64_as_bytes(b: &mut Bencher) {
    let mut r = rng();
    b.iter(|| {
        let mut buf = [0; 8];
        r.read_bytes(&mut buf);
        u64::from_le_bytes(buf)
    });
}
```

Results on my machine (Intel i7-6700K, uses AVX2 SIMD backend):

```text
running 4 tests
test read_u32_as_bytes ... bench:           5.03 ns/iter (+/- 0.21)
test read_u32_direct   ... bench:           2.10 ns/iter (+/- 0.04)
test read_u64_as_bytes ... bench:           5.59 ns/iter (+/- 0.27)
test read_u64_direct   ... bench:           2.70 ns/iter (+/- 0.06)
```

Without runtime target feature detection (removing `features = ["std"]`), i.e., with SSE2 backend:

```text
running 4 tests
test read_u32_as_bytes ... bench:           5.21 ns/iter (+/- 0.43)
test read_u32_direct   ... bench:           2.66 ns/iter (+/- 0.31)
test read_u64_as_bytes ... bench:           6.37 ns/iter (+/- 0.48)
test read_u64_direct   ... bench:           3.93 ns/iter (+/- 0.19)
```

